    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kuribayashi rejected the usual Japanese defence of the beach and built defences in depth, with 18 km of tunnels dug by the time of the landing and positions that could be reoccupied after they had been cleared.</li><li>Total American casualties exceeded Japanese casualties, in a ratio of three American casualties for every two Japanese, which was unique among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> battles involving amphibious island landings.</li><li>Whether the island's capture was worth its cost has been disputed since April 1945, and the airfield's value as a base for fighter escorts and for emergency landings by B-29 bombers is still argued over.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The photograph of the flag-raising on Mount Suribachi shows the second of two flag-raisings there on 23 February 1945. The first flag was replaced by a larger one, and the photograph won the Pulitzer Prize for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">Photography</a> in the year it was published.</p></aside>
    </section>

